    <div class="intro">
      <h2>Explaining the Different Types of Watches</h2>
      <p>
        Watches can look similar at a glance, but what’s inside—and how they’re powered—makes a meaningful difference.
        This guide breaks down the most common types of watches in clear, practical terms, so you can understand what you’re wearing (or buying).
      </p>
    </div>

    <section>
      <h3>Mechanical Watches</h3>
      <p>
        Mechanical watches are powered entirely by a spring and a series of gears—no battery involved.
        They require manual winding to keep running and are often appreciated for their craftsmanship and traditional appeal.
        These watches tend to emphasize heritage, precision engineering, and longevity.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h3>Automatic Watches</h3>
      <p>
        Automatic watches are a type of mechanical watch that wind themselves as you wear them.
        Movement from your wrist powers the mechanism, making them convenient while still preserving traditional watchmaking.
        They’re a popular choice for daily wearers who want mechanical character without constant winding.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h3>Quartz Watches</h3>
      <p>
        Quartz watches are powered by a battery and regulated by a quartz crystal.
        They’re known for their accuracy, affordability, and low maintenance.
        Many iconic designs—both dressy and sporty—use quartz movements because of their reliability.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h3>Solar Watches</h3>
      <p>
        Solar watches use light—natural or artificial—to recharge an internal battery.
        They combine the accuracy of quartz with long-term convenience, often running for months in complete darkness once fully charged.
        These are ideal for wearers who want set-it-and-forget-it reliability.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h3>Digital Watches</h3>
      <p>
        Digital watches display time using numbers rather than hands.
        They often include additional features like alarms, timers, and backlighting.
        While utilitarian by nature, many digital watches have become design icons in their own right.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="divider"></div>

    <section>
      <h3>Which Type Is Right for You?</h3>
      <p>
        There’s no universally “best” type of watch—only what fits your lifestyle and preferences.
        Some people value mechanical tradition, others prioritize accuracy or convenience.
        Understanding the differences helps you choose intentionally, rather than by accident.
      </p>
    </section>
